<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nevada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nevada is a state in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, largely within the arid Great Basin, with its capital at Carson City. Acquired from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1848 and first part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a>, it boomed after the discovery of the Comstock Lode <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> deposits in 1859 and was admitted as a state in 1864, during the Civil War. In the twentieth century legal gambling made Las Vegas and Reno famous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nevada.jpg" alt="Nevada" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caleb Moore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nevada.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nevada is a landlocked state in the Western United States. It borders Idaho to the northeast, Oregon to the northwest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> to the southeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> to the east. Almost the whole state lies within the Basin and Range Province, broken up by many north-south mountain ranges with endorheic valleys between them. Nevada comprises most of the Great Basin as well as a large part of the Mojave Desert.</p><p>The Sierra Nevada lies in the rain shadow to the west, and Nevada is the driest state in the country. The Humboldt River crosses the north from east to west and ends in the Humboldt Sink near Lovelock. The Walker, Truckee and Carson rivers run eastward from the Sierra Nevada and end in Walker Lake, Pyramid Lake and the Carson Sink. The Colorado River forms much of the boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and drains much of the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest inhabitants included the Goshute, Southern Paiute, Mohave and Washoe peoples. Francisco Garcés was the first European in the area, which was claimed as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">Viceroyalty of New Spain</a>. It became part of the province of Alta California in 1804, when the Californias were split. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a> was won in 1821, Alta California became a territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> with a small population.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexed the area in 1848 after its victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a>, and in 1850 it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> territories. The discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> at the Comstock Lode in 1859 caused a population boom and became an impetus for the creation of Nevada Territory out of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a> in 1861. Nevada became the 36th state on 31 October 1864, the second of two states added to the Union during the Civil War after West Virginia. For that reason it is called the Battle Born State.</p><p>In 1940, with just over 110,000 people, Nevada was by far the least populated state. Legalized gambling and lenient marriage and divorce laws then made it a major tourist destination. Las Vegas grew from a gulch of 100 people in 1900 to 10,000 by 1950 and 100,000 by 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is Carson City and the largest city is Las Vegas. The state's name is Spanish for snowy, and its official nickname is the Silver State because of silver's historical importance to its economy. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,104,614 people, nearly three-quarters of them in Clark County, which contains the Las Vegas-Paradise metropolitan area. Tourism is the largest employer, and mining remains a substantial sector, with gold the most valuable mineral. About 80 per cent of the land is managed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> federal government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nevada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Northern Paiutes of Mason Valley, in what became the state of <b>Nevada</b>, were known collectively as the Tövusidökadö (literally 'grass bulb eaters') at the time of European contact.</blockquote><a href="/movement/ghost-dance">Ghost Dance · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghost Dance songs belong to a ceremony that began among the Northern Paiute of Mason Valley, in what became the state of <b>Nevada</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ghost-dance">Ghost Dance songs</a></li><li><blockquote>Mexico ceded about half its territory, including California, <b>Nevada</b>, Utah and most of Arizona and New Mexico, to the United States for 15 million dollars, and recognised Texas as American.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Two days before leaving office, President James Buchanan signed acts organising the <b>Nevada</b> and Dakota territories.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>An Act of Congress established Yosemite National Park in California's Sierra <b>Nevada</b>, protecting the high country around the Yosemite Valley.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-1">1 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nevada</b> was admitted to the Union during the Civil War, with Republicans keen on its votes for Lincoln's re-election and support for the 13th Amendment.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
